# launch

## mux
- label: tracequest serve integrates the multiplexer at boot — when tmux is available the startup ensures the tracequest tmux session exists (creating it detached when absent, reusing it untouched when present) and logs one stdout line naming the tmux session, so `tmux attach -t tracequest` from a real terminal shows exactly the runs the web UI manages; when tmux is NOT resolvable serve still boots, serves every pre-existing route unchanged, never spawns a tmux process, and the launch APIs report the multiplexer unavailable instead of erroring
  id: labt
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'serve ensures the tmux session at boot|serve boots without tmux and reports mux unavailable' test/bin/tracequest-launch-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, launch, launch-s0, mux, serve, implemented]

## api
- label: POST /api/runs with JSON body {agent, cwd, prompt} starts a Run — a new window in the tracequest tmux session executing the detected agent's registry argv (prompt included per that agent's argv shape, cwd as the window's working directory), with remain-on-exit switched on so a finished agent's final screen stays viewable, and run metadata stored in tmux window user options named tq_agent, tq_cwd, and tq_started in tmux's "@"-prefixed user-option namespace — and responds with JSON carrying the run id, which IS the tmux window id; tracequest keeps no run table of its own
  id: larc
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'POST /api/runs starts a run in a tmux window' test/bin/tracequest-launch-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, launch, launch-s1, api, implemented]
- label: GET /api/runs lists every Run as {id, agent, cwd, startedAt, status} where status is the generating word "running", "idle", or "exited" (running only while detectLiveSessions / runIsLive would count the launched run — pending first recording or a linked generating recording — never tmux pane_dead alone), only windows carrying the tq_agent user option count as runs (a window a human opened by hand in the same session is not a run), and because ALL run identity lives in tmux the runs survive tracequest restarts — a freshly started serve process lists the same runs with the same ids, and an exited run stays listed (remain-on-exit) until it is killed
  id: larl
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'GET /api/runs lists runs with live status|runs survive a serve restart' test/bin/tracequest-launch-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, launch, launch-s1, api, implemented]
- label: GET /api/runs/snapshot?id=<runId> answers JSON {status, html} where status is the generating word "running"|"idle"|"exited" (same bit as GET /api/runs, never tmux-alive as running) and html is the run's current screen — tmux capture-pane -e -p output converted SGR to HTML by src/render/ansi-html.js — and the route bypasses the route cache entirely so successive ~600ms polls observe fresh terminal content; an id naming no run window answers 404 rather than an empty screen
  id: lasn
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'snapshot returns the captured screen as html' test/bin/tracequest-launch-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, launch, launch-s2, api, implemented]
- label: POST /api/runs/kill {id} kills the run's tmux window — afterwards the window is gone from tmux list-windows and GET /api/runs no longer lists the run; killing works for running and exited runs alike (it is also how a finished remain-on-exit window is dismissed), and an unknown id answers 404 without touching tmux
  id: lakl
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'kill removes the run window' test/bin/tracequest-launch-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, launch, launch-s2, api, implemented]
- label: POST /api/runs/input {id, text?, key?} forwards input into a run via tmux send-keys — text is sent literally (send-keys -l, so tmux never interprets it as key names), key names exactly one special key from the closed set Enter, C-c, Escape, Up, Down, Tab sent as a separate non-literal send-keys AFTER the text (so {text, key:"Enter"} types a line and submits it), at least one of text/key is required, and a key outside the set answers 400 with nothing sent — the key set is a server-side allowlist, never a passthrough of arbitrary tmux key syntax
  id: lain
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'input sends text and Enter to a run|input rejects an unknown key name' test/bin/tracequest-launch-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, launch, launch-s5, api, implemented]

## security
- label: POST /api/runs launches ONLY allowlisted agents — the agent field must name a registry entry currently detected on PATH (unknown or undetected agent answers 400 and no window is created) and cwd must be an existing directory (400 otherwise) — so the request chooses among detected agents and supplies prompt/cwd data but never a command line; the executed argv always comes from the registry shape
  id: laal
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'unknown agents are rejected with 400|missing cwd is rejected with 400' test/bin/tracequest-launch-integration.test.js
  tags: [spec, launch, launch-s1, security, implemented]

## continue
- label: a resumed claude (or grok) run is deterministic by construction: the launcher mints a fresh fork uuid, passes it as --session-id alongside --resume <sourceId> --fork-session, and persists it as the tq_session_id window option — so the existing tier-0 spawn-identity linker binds the run to the forked recording <uuid>.jsonl and nothing else ever (attribution sid), and because fork semantics copy the prior conversation into the new recording the run's chat shows the continued session's full context followed by the new turns; agents without spawn identity (codex, cursor-agent, opencode) resume through the probe tier plus resumed-run content identity (tq_fork_prompt corroboration and the freshness-gated tq_resumed_path source fallback — fact clrs) and honestly pend until one of those tiers produces evidence — a resume never pre-claims the source file for them (the source links only after its recording's mtime moves at/after the resumed run's start) and never bare-guesses an unrelated fresh file
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-name-pattern continue test/bin/tracequest-launch-integration.test.js
  tags: [launch, continue-resume, chat-live, implemented]
- label: Continue affordances appear exactly where a session can actually be continued, and nowhere else — and every one is a COMPOSER whose typing IS the continue, never a bare button: an exited (or killed) run's chat re-arms its own input card into continue mode (data-mode=continue, fork-semantics chip, 'Send a follow-up — continues in a new run' placeholder, Send-key control retired) gated on canResume AND a linked recording; the read-only external-session chat (/run?session=) renders a composer-card continue form under its observer card when the source's mapped agent is detected resume-capable with mux available; the /view page floats a mini continue composer beside the live-run/watch chips; and dashboard session rows plus EXITED run rows open an inline row composer from their Continue control, gated on GET /api/agents. Agents or sources without a resume mechanism get NO composer — honest absence. Every affordance POSTs {resumeSession, prompt} to /api/runs (empty submit continues without a message) and lands the user in the NEW run's chat at /run?id=<newId> with the follow-up already delivered, the header showing a 'continued from <hash>' provenance link back to the source session view
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test test/browser/continue-resume.playwright.test.js
  tags: [launch, continue-resume, chat-ui, implemented]
- label: Typing IS the continue, end-to-end in a real browser: on an EXITED run's chat the live continue composer accepts a follow-up and submit lands in the NEW run's chat where the transcript eventually shows the prior context, the typed follow-up (delivered via the resume argv's positional prompt), and the resumed reply — with an honest interim on the landing page (the follow-up renders as a dashed pending user bubble sourced from the sessionStorage handoff or the persisted tq_prompt snippet, retiring only once the fork recording carries the message); the dashboard row's inline composer and the observer composer complete the same one-action flow
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 test/browser/continue-resume.playwright.test.js
  tags: [launch, continue-resume, chat-ui, chat-live, implemented]
